               models are flexible (enabling application to a wide variety of
               locations and contexts), can be run using freely available data
               and are provided as an R package. We apply a mesoclimate model to
               the Lizard Peninsula in Cornwall to provide hourly estimates of
               air temperature at resolution of 100 m for the period Jan‐Dec
               2010. A microclimate model is then applied to a 1 km2 region of
               the Lizard Peninsula, Caerthillean Valley (49.969°N, 5.215°W), to
               provide hourly estimates of near‐ground air temperature at
               resolution of 1 m2 during May 2010. Our models reveal substantial
               spatial variation in near‐ground temperatures, driven principally
               by variation in topography and, at the microscale, by vegetation
